djaq Adcock was 6 years old when he died  from undiagnosed septic shock at  Leicester Royal Infirmary junior doctor  hadiza Barra Garber was convicted of  gross negligence manslaughter for  mistakes she made in his treatment but  today she was cleared to return to  medical practice Sky News was with  Jack's mother Nicola when the ruling was  handed down absolute joke so where's the  public confidence where does that leave  people where's the patient's safety so  my son died due to neglect from her and  she's allowed to go back to a hospital  with a gross negligence manslaughter  charge and treat or the patients  absolutely disgusted what happened here  in 2011 has had far-reaching  implications and raises profound  questions of medical ethics chiefly this  how should doctors be held accountable  for their errors and should anyone with  a manslaughter conviction be allowed to  return to work dr. barber Garber's  backers say she is truly sorry and the  decision is the right one she spent many  hours trying to prove herself look into  the errors that she did make apologize  to the Adcock family for her role in  what happened but she wasn't the only  one on duty that day and the hospital  has a lot of accountability here the  case galvanized support from junior  doctors who say that wider pressure on  the NHS contributed dr. Barbara Garber's  mistakes it's tough to see so many  doctors rallying around a doctor who's  been convicted of a crime but that's  because when you look more closely into  the crime you can see the factors that  led to this happening and a lot of  doctors feel like they've been in that  situation trying to provide that care  dr. Barwick Garber has made no comment  but last year after she successfully  appealed against being struck off she  said this I hope but I will be given the  opportunity  to do what I enjoy which is looking  after sick children and  I know that the lessons that I've learnt  from Jack's death will always make me a  better doctor for the Adcox promises  will never be enough  they might say we're going to retrain  her she might become a better doctor  would you know what she's been given a  second chance where's my son  dr. Barbara Garber will return to work  under supervision next year  Paul Kelso sky news
 

